<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- NOT SANDBOXED (Doc 3 §1, ratified 2026-07-30). Two reasons originally: the bundled MCP
	     helper could not ship in a MAS bundle, and Doc 3 §2 made the app own the substrate engine's
	     process. THE FIRST REASON IS GONE — that helper was deleted 2026-08-07 (Doc 4 Phase 3) —
	     and the second alone still decides it: a sandboxed app cannot spawn a subprocess outside
	     its bundle, so `com.apple.security.app-sandbox` and the engine supervisor remain mutually
	     exclusive. SPEC.md's locked-decision row records the move and both dates.

	     WHAT EACH REMAINING KEY IS NOW WORTH, since half of them were sandbox concepts:

	       application-groups   KEPT, and the reason CHANGED with the helper's deletion — it did not
	                            weaken. On macOS the group container is not a sandbox artefact: any
	                            binary signed by this team and carrying this entitlement resolves
	                            it. It used to matter because the unsandboxed helper read index.db
	                            out of the same container. It now matters because `mcp-state.json`
	                            lives there and a workspace vault's manifest records that ABSOLUTE
	                            PATH as its `guard_state`. Drop the key and
	                            `SharedLocations.supportDirectory` falls back to Application
	                            Support, every existing manifest keeps naming the container path,
	                            and the engine finds no heartbeat — so the guard fails closed and
	                            every workspace's own calls stop answering, correctly and for
	                            entirely the wrong reason.
	       device.audio-input   KEPT. TCC, not the sandbox — unaffected either way, and the
	                            microphone prompt is still driven by NSMicrophoneUsageDescription.
	       calendars            KEPT, same reason.
	       network.client       KEPT. Outbound loopback only (the engine on 127.0.0.1:8765 and, when
	                            the operator opts in, a local model server) — inert without the
	                            sandbox but correct if the sandbox ever returns for a flavor.

	     DROPPED: files.user-selected.read-write. It only ever meant anything under the sandbox, and
	     the folder grant it backed is gone with it — an unsandboxed app reaches the operator's
	     transcripts folder by PATH. The stored security-scoped bookmark does not survive the
	     crossing (it resolves as NSCocoaErrorDomain 259 outside the sandbox that wrote it), which
	     is why `ContainerPreferences` carries the path forward and leaves the bookmark behind.

	     DROPPED: print. Nothing here reaches a printer — the one `NSPrintOperation`, in
	     `TranscriptExporter.exportPDF`, runs with `jobDisposition = .save` and no print panel. -->
	<key>com.apple.security.application-groups</key>
	<array>
		<string>6CTH5M9UWZ.com.ronanwood.calltranscriber</string>
	</array>
	<key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.personal-information.calendars</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
